## Architecture

The library has four modules under `pyintelliclima/`:

- **`const.py`**: API base URL, endpoint constants, `REFRESH_DELAY` (5s sleep after commands), and
  the protocol enums. `FanMode`/`FanSpeed` are *setpoints*, with string values that are the register
  byte in decimal. `FanSpeedState`/`FanPreset` plus the `SPEED_FLAG_*` masks describe the *reported*
  running state, which is a different encoding. `ThresholdLevel` is likewise a setpoint: the
  reported threshold registers add the `THRESHOLD_FLAG_ADVANCED` bit on top of it.
- **`intelliclima_types.py`**: Dataclasses for all request/response types. `IntelliClimaVMCBase`
  holds the status fields shared by the VMC family; `IntelliClimaECO2` and `IntelliClimaECO3` are
  siblings on it rather than one subclassing the other, so `isinstance` is exact in both
  directions. They carry only what genuinely differs per generation - `voc_threshold` and
  `co2_threshold` - since dacite needs one shared field list but only one of those two columns
  is ever populated. `IntelliClimaC800` exists but is
  unverified and not implemented. `IntelliClimaDevices` is the container returned from device
  polling. `decode_fan_state()` turns the reported `mode_state`/`speed_state` registers into a
  `FanState`, and `decode_threshold()` turns a reported `*_thrs` register into a
  `ThresholdSetting`. Both are exposed as properties on `IntelliClimaVMCBase`; prefer those over
  the raw fields, and decode before resending a threshold in a partial write.
- **`api.py`**: The client classes plus low-level helpers:
  - `IntelliClimaAPI`: Top-level client. Handles auth (SHA-256 password hash), fetches house/device
    IDs, and polls all device statuses. Owns `ecocomfort2` and `ecocomfort3` sub-clients.
  - `_IntelliClimaVMCAPI`: Shared base for both VMC generations. They speak the same command
    protocol over identically named endpoints, so the concrete classes differ only in
    `_endpoint_prefix` (`eco` vs `eco3`). After each command it sleeps `REFRESH_DELAY` seconds to
    let the device update before the next status poll.
  - `IntelliClimaEcocomfort2API` / `IntelliClimaEcocomfort3API`: the two concrete clients.
  - `create_mode_speed_command()` and friends: Build the binary command frames, encoded as hex
    strings with CRC8-NRSC5 checksums. The frame format was reverse-engineered; the CRC polynomial
    is 0x31, init 0xFF. `tests/test_vendor_frames.py` checks them against a port of the vendor
    app's own builders.
- **`__init__.py`**: Public exports.

## Key API Details

- Auth: POST to `user/login/{username}/{sha256_password}` with a device registration JSON body.
  Returns `token` and `id` used as `TOKEN`/`TOKENID` headers on all subsequent requests. A
  rejected login is an HTTP 200 whose body carries a non-`OK` `status` and an `error` of
  `NO_USERNAME`/`NO_PASSWORD`, which is why login alone skips `post_to_session`'s status check.
- Device discovery: POST to `casa/elenco3/{user_id}`. The response's own `ecoIDs`/`eco3IDs`
  arrays name the ECOCOMFORT 2.0 and ECOCOMFORT 3 devices; anything in `houses` that appears in
  neither is a family we don't implement and is logged and skipped.
- Status polling: POST to `sync/cronos400` with a list of device IDs. Each `data[]` item names
  its own family in `model`, a JSON string that has to be expanded before `dacite` sees it. Any
  such nested field can arrive as JSON `null`, so expand it through `_load_json_field`.
- Commands: POST to `eco/send/` (or `eco3/send/`) with `{"trama": "<hex command string>"}`.
- The server's own JSON uses master/slave wording (`role`, `slv_rot`, `slv_addr`, `isMaster`,
  `*MasterIDs`). Dataclass fields mirror those keys verbatim because dacite matches by field
  name, but our own API surface says main/satellite instead - follow that in new code.
- `mode_set`/`speed_set` are the last *commanded* values and are not what the unit is doing. The
  vendor app displays `mode_state`/`speed_state` instead; read `device.fan_state` for those.
  Writes that preserve "the current speed" should still use `speed_set`, or a temporary boost
  or night override gets commanded back as a permanent setting.

## Dependencies

- `aiohttp`: All HTTP calls are async; callers must provide a `ClientSession`.
- `dacite`: Used to deserialize the status polling response dicts into `IntelliClimaECO` dataclasses.
